       SECTION 1: WHAT ARE AUSTRALIA'S TIME ZONES?
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="what-are-australia-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="What Are Australia's Time Zones">
    <h2>1. What Are Australia's Time Zones?</h2>
    <p>
      Australia is a massive continent spanning over 4,000 kilometers from east to west. 
      This vast horizontal footprint covers nearly 40 degrees of longitude, making standard time zones necessary to align local activities with standard daylight patterns. 
      Without standard time, clocks in Perth would be completely out of sync with the solar cycles of Sydney.
    </p>
    
    <div class="tip-box">
      <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Under the Australian Constitution, timekeeping and Daylight Saving Time legislation are the sole responsibility of individual state and territory governments. 
      This decentralization explains why boundaries do not always align with state lines and why some states observe seasonal changes while others do not.
    </div>

    <p>
      This regional division creates unique scheduling dynamics. 
      For example, during winter, the country operates on three standard offsets: UTC+8, UTC+9:30, and UTC+10. 
      During summer, because only some states choose to shift their clocks, the mainland divides into five distinct offsets: UTC+8, UTC+9:30, UTC+10, UTC+10:30, and UTC+11. 
      These standard alignments are officially registered in the IANA Time Zone Database and referenced against global standards like <a href="/gmt-vs-utc/">Coordinated Universal Time (UTC)</a> and <a href="/what-is-gmt/">Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 1: Australia Time Zone Map -->
