    <p>
      Navigating these divisions is essential for national business operations, interstate logistics, flight schedules, and telecommunications. 
      For professionals managing schedules across different countries, aligning Australian hours with other regions (like the US or Canada) requires close attention to seasonal changes. 
      You can compare these regional corridors with our detailed <a href="/usa-time-zones/">USA Time Zones guide</a> and <a href="/canada-time-zones/">Canada Time Zones guide</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 2: HISTORY OF AUSTRALIAN TIME ZONES
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="history-of-canadian-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="History of Australian Time Zones">
    <h2>2. History of Australian Time Zones</h2>
    <p>
      Before the late 19th century, Australian colonies observed local mean time based on the meridian of their local observatory. 
      Every major town set its own clocks, which made train scheduling and telegraph communication extremely difficult. 
      As railway networks expanded across colonial borders, standardizing time became a priority for colonial administrators.
    </p>
    
    <div class="did-you-know">
      <h4>Did You Know?</h4>
      <p>
        The standardization of time in Australia occurred during the colonial conference in 1892. 
        It was agreed to adopt three standard time zones based on standard meridians: 120° E (Western Australia), 135° E (South Australia and Northern Territory), and 150° E (Queensland, New South Wales, Victoria, and Tasmania). 
        The colonies officially enacted this agreement in 1895 through individual Standard Time Acts, several years before the Australian Federation in 1901.
      </p>
    </div>

    <p>
      Historical adjustments continued after Federation. 
      In 1899, South Australia decided to shift its standard meridian to 142.5° E, moving its clock 30 minutes forward to UTC+9:30. 
      This was done to bring Adelaide's clocks closer to the eastern states, but it created Australia's famous half-hour offset. 
      The Northern Territory inherited this half-hour offset, continuing to use it even after administrative separation.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 3: Australia DST Timeline -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/australia-dst-timeline.webp" 
