        decoding="async" 
        width="800" 
        height="400" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 3: Australian Timekeeping and DST History.</strong> Timeline of key legislative developments in Australian time synchronization, from standard time acts in 1895 to modern daylight saving agreements.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time rules evolved independently across states. 
      During World War I and II, the federal government enforced nationwide DST to conserve energy. 
      However, in peacetime, states resumed independent control. 
      In 1971, the eastern states (NSW, VIC, QLD, TAS, and ACT) ran a trial of DST. 
      Following the trial, NSW, VIC, TAS, and SA decided to adopt it permanently, while Queensland rejected the seasonal shift, creating the modern summer timezone division.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 3: AUSTRALIA'S PRIMARY TIME ZONES (MAINLAND)
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="primary-timezones" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Australia's Primary Time Zones">
    <h2>3. Australia's Primary Time Zones (Mainland)</h2>
    <p>
      Mainland Australia is governed by three primary standard time zones, calculated relative to Coordinated Universal Time (UTC):
    </p>

    <!-- AWST -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Australian Western Standard Time (AWST)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Standard Offset:</strong> UTC+8 year-round (No Daylight Saving Time)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Perth, Fremantle, Bunbury, Geraldton, Broome.  
        <br /><strong>Coverage:</strong> The entire state of Western Australia.
      </p>
      <p>
        AWST covers Western Australia, the country's resource-rich gateway to Asia. 
        Because Western Australia does not observe Daylight Saving Time, Perth remains on UTC+8 year-round. 
        This keeps WA aligned with major Asian business centers like Singapore, Hong Kong, and Beijing.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business Scheduling Example:</strong> A mining company based in Perth (AWST, UTC+8) hosting a financial audit with a bank in Sydney (AEST, UTC+10 in winter) must manage a 2-hour offset. 
        A meeting scheduled for 9:00 a.m. in Perth corresponds to 11:00 a.m. in Sydney. 
        In summer, when Sydney shifts to AEDT (UTC+11), the difference expands to 3 hours, meaning the same 9:00 a.m. Perth meeting occurs at 12:00 p.m. in Sydney.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Travel Example:</strong> A flight from Sydney to Perth takes approximately 5 hours. 
        If a traveler departs Sydney at 3:00 p.m. AEDT in December (UTC+11), they will land in Perth (UTC+8) at 5:00 p.m. local time, making the travel time feel like only 2 hours.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- ACST / ACDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Australian Central Standard Time (ACST) / Central Daylight Time (ACDT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Standard Offset (Winter):</strong> UTC+9:30 (Australian Central Standard Time - ACST)  
        <br /><strong>Daylight Offset (Summer):</strong> UTC+10:30 (Australian Central Daylight Time - ACDT)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Adelaide, Darwin, Alice Springs, Broken Hill.  
        <br /><strong>Coverage:</strong> South Australia, Northern Territory, and Broken Hill (NSW).
      </p>
      <p>
        The Central Time Zone features a unique half-hour offset. 
        During winter standard time, South Australia and the Northern Territory use ACST (UTC+9:30). 
        During summer, South Australia observes Daylight Saving Time, shifting to ACDT (UTC+10:30). 
        The Northern Territory does not observe DST, remaining on UTC+9:30 year-round.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business Scheduling Example:</strong> A medical researcher in Adelaide (ACDT, UTC+10:30 in summer) calling a regional clinic in Darwin (ACST, UTC+9:30 year-round) must account for a 1-hour shift. 
        A call at 10:00 a.m. in Adelaide occurs at 9:00 a.m. in Darwin.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Travel Example:</strong> Driving north from Adelaide to Darwin via the Stuart Highway involves crossing from South Australia into the Northern Territory. 
        If traveling in December, crossing the state border requires setting dashboard clocks back 1 hour, as South Australia observes DST while the Northern Territory does not.
      </p>
    </article>

    <!-- AEST / AEDT -->
    <article class="method-card" style="margin-bottom: 2rem;">
      <h3>Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) / Eastern Daylight Time (AEDT)</h3>
      <p>
        <strong>Standard Offset (Winter):</strong> UTC+10 (Australian Eastern Standard Time - AEST)  
        <br /><strong>Daylight Offset (Summer):</strong> UTC+11 (Australian Eastern Daylight Time - AEDT)  
        <br /><strong>Major Cities:</strong> Sydney, Melbourne, Brisbane, Canberra, Hobart, Gold Coast.  
        <br /><strong>Coverage:</strong> New South Wales (except Broken Hill), Victoria, Queensland, Tasmania, and the ACT.
      </p>
      <p>
        The Eastern Time Zone covers Australia's eastern seaboard, housing the majority of the population and the country's primary economic hubs. 
        In winter, the entire eastern seaboard is aligned on AEST (UTC+10). 
        In summer, New South Wales, Victoria, Tasmania, and the ACT observe Daylight Saving Time, moving to AEDT (UTC+11). 
        Queensland does not observe DST, remaining on AEST (UTC+10) year-round. 
        This is comparable to standard transitions like <a href="/est-vs-edt/">EST vs EDT</a> in other global guides.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Business Scheduling Example:</strong> The Australian Securities Exchange (ASX) operates from 10:00 a.m. to 4:00 p.m. Sydney Time. 
        Brokers in Brisbane (AEST, UTC+10 year-round) must start trading at 9:00 a.m. local time during summer to align with the opening bell in Sydney.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Travel Example:</strong> Crossing the border from Coolangatta (Queensland, AEST) to Tweed Heads (New South Wales, AEDT) on foot during summer involves a 1-hour time jump. 
        Walking across the street can literally change the hour on your phone, requiring close attention from local commuters.
      </p>
    </article>

