  <!-- ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
       SECTION 4: DAYLIGHT SAVING TIME (DST) IN AUSTRALIA
       ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════ -->
  <section id="daylight-saving" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Daylight Saving Time in Australia">
    <h2>4. Daylight Saving Time (DST) in Australia</h2>
    <p>
      Daylight Saving Time (DST) is observed in South Australia, New South Wales, Victoria, Tasmania, and the Australian Capital Territory. 
      In these regions, clocks advance by one hour on the first Sunday of October and return to standard time on the first Sunday of April. 
      This is designed to extend evening daylight during the warmer summer months. 
      To understand this concept further, read our guide on <a href="/daylight-saving-time-explained/">Daylight Saving Time Explained</a>.
    </p>

    <!-- Visual Asset 5: AEST vs AEDT Comparison -->
    <figure style="margin: 2.5rem 0; text-align: center; border: 1px solid var(--line); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 12px; background: var(--panel);">
      <img 
        src="/assets/aest-vs-aedt-comparison.webp" 
        alt="AEST vs AEDT Comparison Graphic showing offset changes" 
        title="AEST vs AEDT Comparison" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="1000" 
        height="500" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 5: AEST vs AEDT Comparison.</strong> Graphical explanation showing how the eastern states shift from Australian Eastern Standard Time (AEST) to Eastern Daylight Time (AEDT) in October.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      However, Western Australia, Queensland, and the Northern Territory do not observe DST. 
      This creates a summer timezone split across the continent:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>The Latitudinal Factor:</strong> In northern Australia (Queensland and Northern Territory), daylight hours remain relatively constant year-round due to the tropical latitude. 
        Adding DST would push sunset past 8:00 p.m. in the summer, which is unpopular due to extreme heat.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Agricultural Opposition:</strong> Farming communities in Western Australia and Queensland oppose DST. 
        They argue that changing clocks disrupts harvest schedules, livestock routines, and coordinates poorly with natural daylight cycles.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <div class="warning-box">
      <strong>Warning:</strong> During the summer DST period, Australia operates on five distinct offsets rather than three. 
      Brisbane remains on UTC+10 (AEST), while Sydney shifts to UTC+11 (AEDT). 
      Ensure your calendars account for this state division between October and April.
    </div>
  </section>

