      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Remote Work Coordination:</strong> Collaborative hours must be set carefully. 
        During summer, when it is 9:00 a.m. in Perth (AWST), it is already 12:00 p.m. in Sydney (AEDT). 
        To hold meetings during standard working hours, teams must target the 12:00 p.m. to 4:00 p.m. AEDT window, which corresponds to 9:00 a.m. to 1:00 p.m. AWST in Western Australia.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Financial Markets:</strong> The Australian Securities Exchange (ASX) in Sydney dictates national financial activities. 
        Brokers on the West Coast must begin their daily work at 7:00 a.m. local time during summer to ensure they can trade from the opening bell.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>National Support Operations:</strong> Support centers must align their shifts to cover the national business window. 
        Operating hours starting at 8:00 a.m. AEDT in Melbourne will only cover Perth customers up to 5:00 p.m. AWST, requiring shift handovers.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Logistics and Supply Chain:</strong> National freight systems use Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) or Eastern Standard Time (AEST) to coordinate transcontinental rail transport and flight schedules.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

