        title="Programming with Australian Time Zones" 
        loading="lazy" 
        decoding="async" 
        width="1000" 
        height="500" 
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;"
      />
      <figcaption class="infographic-caption">
        <strong>Figure 9: Developer Timezone Database Workflow.</strong> Structural diagram demonstrating the best practice of storing timezone-agnostic Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) in databases and translating to local IANA zone names in user interfaces.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h3>1. Storing Dates in UTC</h3>
    <p>
      Always store timestamps in Coordinated Universal Time (UTC) using the ISO 8601 standard formatting: `YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SSZ`. 
      Do not store local timestamps directly, as it makes database queries across SA, WA, and NSW nearly impossible.
    </p>

    <h3>2. Use IANA Identifiers</h3>
    <p>
      Do not hardcode offsets (like +10 or +8) in your codebase. 
      Offsets change seasonally and can be modified by legislation. Instead, use the official IANA Time Zone Database names. 
      Examples for Australia include:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>`Australia/Perth` (AWST)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Adelaide` (ACST/ACDT)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Darwin` (ACST year-round)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Sydney` (AEST/AEDT)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Brisbane` (AEST year-round)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Hobart` (AEST/AEDT in Tasmania)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Lord_Howe` (Lord Howe Island)</li>
      <li>`Australia/Eucla` (Central Western Time)</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>3. JavaScript Localization Example</h3>
    <p>
      To display a database UTC timestamp in Sydney's local daylight time (AEDT), utilize the native `Intl.DateTimeFormat` API:
    </p>

    <pre style="background: var(--panel); padding: 1.5rem; border-radius: 8px; border: 1px solid var(--line); overflow-x: auto; color: var(--text); font-family: monospace;">{`const utcDate = new Date('2026-07-15T12:00:00Z');
const formatter = new Intl.DateTimeFormat('en-AU', {
  timeZone: 'Australia/Sydney',
  dateStyle: 'medium',
  timeStyle: 'long'
});
console.log(formatter.format(utcDate)); 
// Output will render in Sydney local time (AEST/AEDT)`}</pre>
  </section>

