  <section id="common-mistakes" class="content-section reveal" aria-label="Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid">
    <h2>10. Common Time Zone Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
    <p>
      Working, coding, or traveling across Australia involves navigating several unique timezone rules. 
      Avoid these common pitfalls:
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Neglecting Half-Hour Offsets:</strong> Many automated schedulers assume all time zones use whole-hour offsets. 
        Forgetting that South Australia and the Northern Territory use a half-hour offset (UTC+9:30) can lead to scheduling errors for Adelaide and Darwin.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Confusing AEST with AEDT:</strong> Do not use these terms interchangeably. 
        AEST is standard winter time (UTC+10), while AEDT is summer daylight time (UTC+11). 
        Using AEST in summer will result in meetings being scheduled one hour late.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Assuming Queensland Changes Clocks:</strong> Queensland remains on AEST (UTC+10) year-round. 
        Ensure your calendars do not shift Brisbane clocks in October or April.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Forgetting the 45-Minute Eucla Offset:</strong> If your business operates logistics along the Eyre Highway, ensure you account for Eucla's UTC+8:45 offset, which is observed by local roadhouses.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

